    <link name="right_track_retract_mount">
      <pose>-0.05 -0.35 -0.2 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.1</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.001</ixx>
          <iyy>0.001</iyy>
          <izz>0.001</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="right_track_retract_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>right_track_retract_mount</child>
      <!-- Axis negated relative to the left joint's <xyz>1 0 0</xyz> (real bug
           found in final review, fixed here): left/right track mounts are
           mirror images across the hull's centerline (y flipped), so rotating
           both about the SAME +x axis direction swung them in physically
           opposite directions -- the left track outboard-and-up (correct) but
           the right track INBOARD-and-up, tucking it toward the hull
           centerline instead of retracting outward. self_collide is off so
           this never crashed, and "the joints moved 0<->1.4 rad" verification
           still passed, which is why this survived every earlier live check. -->
      <axis>
        <xyz>-1 0 0</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>0.0</lower>
          <upper>1.4</upper>
          <effort>50</effort>
          <velocity>1.0</velocity>
        </limit>
      </axis>
    </joint>
    <link name="right_track_strut">
      <pose relative_to="right_track_retract_mount">0 -0.015 -0.075 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.15</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.0005</ixx><iyy>0.0005</iyy><izz>0.0005</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="right_track_strut_visual">
        <geometry><box><size>0.08 0.03 0.15</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</ambient><diffuse>0.25 0.25 0.25 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="right_track_strut_collision">
        <geometry><box><size>0.08 0.03 0.15</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>
    <joint name="right_track_strut_fixed" type="fixed">
      <parent>right_track_retract_mount</parent>
      <child>right_track_strut</child>
    </joint>
    <link name="right_track">
      <pose relative_to="right_track_retract_mount">0 -0.03 -0.15 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>1.5</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.02</ixx>
          <iyy>0.05</iyy>
          <izz>0.05</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="col">
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.6 0.12 0.15</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <!-- REAL BUG FOUND AND FIXED: this box had zero <surface><friction>
             at all (isotropic gz-sim/DART default), despite gz-sim's own
             reference example (worlds/tracked_vehicle_simple.sdf,
             gz-sim8) requiring anisotropic friction (fdir1 + mu/mu2) on
             every track collision for the TrackedVehicle system's belt
             simulation to convert commanded track speed into correct real
             motion. Without it, commanded forward motion produced mostly
             LATERAL real motion (measured live at 71-92deg off-axis,
             matching this file's own previously-documented "KNOWN OPEN
             ISSUE" on the ArduPilotPlugin block above -- same bug,
             different investigation). fdir1="0 1 0" (Y = across-track/
             skid axis, moderate mu) matches the reference exactly --
             this box is already unrotated with its long (along-track/
             propulsion) axis on local +X, matching TrackController's own
             track_orientation "1 0 0" below, so no rotation-compensation
             was needed unlike the reference's own rotated collision pose.
             CONFIRMED LIVE (fixed): direction went from 71-92deg off-axis
             to ~6-11deg off-axis, i.e. genuinely forward now.

             mu2=150 (the reference's own value) initially caused real,
             measured RTF collapse (0.6+ -> ~0.09) and near-zero real
             displacement on THIS world's dense, irregular cave-floor
             mesh -- the reference world's ground is a flat <plane>,
             and box-vs-uneven-mesh contact at high friction is a known
             ODE solver stiffness source independent of box length
             (confirmed: splitting this box into 3 segments matching the
             reference's own left/right_track_collision/_collision_1/
             _collision_2 structure did NOT help, RTF still collapsed --
             reverted, not worth the complexity). Real fix: raised
             cavex_world.world's ODE solver iterations 50 (gz-sim
             default) -> 200 (see that file's own <physics><ode><solver>
             comment) -- CONFIRMED LIVE this restores full stability at
             mu2=150 (RTF held ~0.6-0.7 under sustained driving, same as
             mu2=5/50, no collapse at any of the three).

             REAL ROOT CAUSE of the "speed identical across mu2=5/50/150"
             finding (found while testing mu2=300): every one of those
             "gz-transport bypass" tests was silently confounded --
             gazebo_tracked_vehicle.launch.py's own track_cmd_vel_bridge.py
             (part of the real ArduPilot control path) was STILL RUNNING
             during every "bypass" test and continuously republishing
             ArduPilot's own near-idle reported velocity onto the exact
             same gz-transport /model/.../cmd_vel topic the bypass test
             was ALSO manually publishing to -- two competing publishers
             on one topic, with the bridge's tiny value dominating.
             CONFIRMED: killing track_cmd_vel_bridge.py before testing
             (genuine isolation) at mu2=300 -- track_cmd_vel reported
             EXACTLY the commanded 1.0 (was 0.02-0.03 before, regardless
             of command magnitude), and real displacement was 4.91m over
             a 10s wall-clock test (~0.98 m/s sim-time-normalized at
             RTF~0.5) -- i.e. essentially the FULL commanded speed, with
             correct direction. Friction/contact physics were NEVER the
             real speed bottleneck at any mu2 tested -- the entire
             "~3-4% of commanded" finding above was a test-methodology
             artifact, not a real physics limitation. mu2=300 confirmed
             genuinely correct and performant; kept as the tuned value.
             The REAL remaining gap for driving via the actual ArduPilot
             path (not this bypass) is ArduPilot's own Rover GUIDED-mode
             velocity-control tuning (CRUISE_SPEED/CRUISE_THROTTLE/
             ATC_SPEED_P in rover.parm) -- a completely different
             subsystem, not gz-sim physics at all. -->
        <surface>
          <friction>
            <ode>
              <mu>0.7</mu>
              <mu2>300</mu2>
              <fdir1>0 1 0</fdir1>
            </ode>
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
