    </link>
    <joint name="right_track_fixed" type="fixed">
      <parent>right_track_retract_mount</parent>
      <child>right_track</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- sensors -->
    <link name="imu_link">
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>0.1</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0001</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.0001</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.0001</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <sensor name="imu_sensor" type="imu">
        <pose degrees="true">0 0 0 180 0 0</pose>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>1000.0</update_rate>
        <!-- REAL BUG FOUND AND FIXED: no override here meant gz-sim stamped
             the bridged /imu message's frame_id from the scene graph's own
             scoped name (world/model/link/sensor path), not the plain link
             name robot_state_publisher's real TF tree uses -- confirmed
             live via rtabmap's own repeated "does not exist" lookupTransform
             errors for that exact scoped name. Same gz-sim8 gotcha already
             fixed for lidar_link/camera_link_optical above -- see their own
             comments. -->
        <gz_frame_id>imu_link</gz_frame_id>
      </sensor>
    </link>
    <joint name="imu_joint" type="revolute">
      <child>imu_link</child>
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>0</lower>
          <upper>0</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>1.0</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>


    <!-- NavSat/GPS: investigated and removed. navsat_link/navsat_sensor were
         orphaned -- not wired into ArduPilotPlugin below (which only
         declares <imuName>, and gets its position/velocity for SITL's own
         internal GPS/EKF synthesis straight from Gazebo's link pose over
         the FDM socket, never from a GPS sensor plugin) and never bridged
         to ROS anywhere in gazebo_tracked_vehicle_bridge.yaml. Confirmed
         zero consumers before deleting: dead weight that only added a
         SDF-schema parser warning and a small amount of unused sim
         overhead. This is also a cave-exploration vehicle underground,
         where a real GPS fix is unavailable anyway -- the sensor was
         leftover from the original marine BlueBoat model this hull is
         based on and never applied to this repurposing. -->

    <!-- Lidar/camera (added post-Task-7 review, Finding 1: Task 4 never gave
         this hull any perception sensors, only imu/navsat -- Task 10's
         RTAB-Map hard-depends on /lidar/points + /camera/color/*). Native
         SDF <sensor> blocks directly under a fixed-mounted <link>, no
         <gazebo reference=...> wrapper -- that wrapper is URDF-to-SDF-xacro
         syntax only and this file is plain SDF already (same convention
         this file's own ros2_control/plugin comments already establish).
         Real values (16-beam 360 deg gpu_lidar @10Hz, range 0.4-12m (Task 10's
         self-occlusion fix raised the min from an earlier 0.3, see that
         <min> value below); camera
         30Hz 800x800 R8G8B8) and the real proven gz-sim8 gotcha
         (<gz_frame_id> required or gz-sim stamps frame_id from the scene
         graph instead of the link name) both carried over from the
         a prior project vehicle's real, working sensor URDF blocks -- same
         sensor types, same requirement, re-verified live for this hull
         rather than assumed.
         Mount heights picked from blueboat_hull_collision.stl's real
         bounding box (x:[-0.60,0.59] y:[-0.45,0.45] z:[-0.376,0] relative to
         base_link -- hull collision top sits at z=0): both sensors sit at
         z>=0.15, clear of the hull collision mesh and the track assemblies
         below. Camera mounted at the bow (+x, matching the hull's forward
         extent and the +x-is-forward drive convention TrackedVehicle/
         track_cmd_vel_bridge.py already use post-frame-fix). -->
    <link name="lidar_link">
      <!-- Raised from 0.4 to 0.55 (Task 10 lidar-self-occlusion fix): real vertex-transformed
           bounding box of the aerial/antenna mast (blueboat_aerial.dae) is x[0.170,0.192]
           y[-0.375,-0.353] z[0.004,0.318] -- r~0.406m from the mount, top at z=0.318. At the
           old 0.4m mount that top was only 11.4 degrees below horizontal (within the +/-15deg
           vertical FOV), so it self-hit. At 0.55m the same point is 29.7 degrees below
           horizontal -- clear, with margin. All other remaining superstructure (hull, frame_asm,
           crosstube, hatch_asm) tops out at z<=0.091, at r>=0.214m -- angle to those from a
           0.55m mount is >58 degrees, well outside the cone. (blueboat_flag.dae, the one piece
           that couldn't be cleared by any reasonable mount height, was removed above instead.) -->
      <pose relative_to="base_link">0 0 0.55 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.1</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0001</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.0001</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.0001</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <sensor name="lidar_sensor" type="gpu_lidar">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <visualize>false</visualize>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <topic>lidar/points</topic>
        <gz_frame_id>lidar_link</gz_frame_id>
        <ray>
          <scan>
            <!-- Horizontal samples: was raised 360 -> 720 (real request at the
                 time: match OpenRobotics' coro_hd2_sensor_config_2 Fuel model's
                 lidar spec). Reverted back to 360 after being suspected as a
                 navigation-slowdown cause -- but a direct A/B re-measurement
                 (360 vs 720, same scene) showed RTAB-Map's own Conversion step
                 STILL averaged ~0.49-0.51s/cycle either way: point count was
                 NOT the driver. Real cause, confirmed via `nproc`(8)/loadavg
                 (~17, ie ~2x oversubscribed) vs summed process %CPU (~355%,
                 well under 800% capacity): scheduling contention under WSL2
                 virtualization, not any single ROS parameter -- icp_odometry
                 alone (single-threaded ICP) runs ~78% of a core, gz sim ~148%.
                 Left at 360 anyway (this project's original, long-validated
                 baseline, and strictly no worse than 720), but this is NOT a
                 fix for the Conversion-time bottleneck, which remains open and
                 environmental. 1800 (CoRo's own exact value) was already tried
                 and rejected earlier for a different reason (broke SLAM
                 bootstrap entirely). Kept this vehicle's own already-justified
                 min/max range (0.4-30m, see below), unrelated to this
                 horizontal-resolution question. -->
            <horizontal>
              <samples>360</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-3.14159</min_angle>
              <max_angle>3.14159</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <samples>16</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-0.2618</min_angle>
              <max_angle>0.2618</max_angle>
            </vertical>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <!-- min tightened from 0.3 to 0.4 (Task 10 lidar-self-occlusion fix): filters
                 remaining close-range self-hit noise without excluding real near-range cave
                 features (cave walls/obstacles can legitimately be within 1-2m). -->
            <min>0.4</min>
            <!-- max extended from 12.0 to 30.0 -- the dry-section corridor (spawn x=-35 to
                 water boundary x=15) is a real ~50m span; 12m only saw the immediate
                 surroundings, giving RTAB-Map/explore_lite very little advance warning of
                 corridor structure. 30m matches real mid-range 3D lidars used in subterranean
                 robotics (e.g. Velodyne Puck/Ouster OS0-class, not a long-range unit), well
                 within gpu_lidar's simulated range accuracy.
                 Doubled again, 30.0 -> 60.0, alongside cave_world's own 2x mesh scale --
                 the same real corridor span is now ~100m, and this is a sensor-range
                 parameter tracking real environment distance (not a vehicle-size
                 parameter), so it needs to double the same way the other cave-scale
                 detection ranges did. -->
            <max>60.0</max>
          </range>
        </ray>
      </sensor>
    </link>
    <joint name="lidar_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>lidar_link</child>
    </joint>

    <link name="camera_link">
      <pose relative_to="base_link">0.55 0 0.15 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.05</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0001</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.0001</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.0001</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <sensor name="camera_sensor" type="rgbd_camera">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>30.0</update_rate>
        <topic>camera/color</topic>
        <!-- REP 103/145 optical convention (z-forward, x-right, y-down),
             not camera_link's own body convention (x-forward, y-left,
             z-up) -- image_geometry::PinholeCameraModel::project3dToPixel
             (sic_slam_node.cpp) requires this. camera_link_optical is a
             zero-translation, pure-rotation child of camera_link, added
             as a static TF in tracked_vehicle_slam.launch.py. -->
        <gz_frame_id>camera_link_optical</gz_frame_id>
        <camera>
          <horizontal_fov>1.3962634</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>800</width>
            <height>800</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>0.02</near>
            <far>300</far>
          </clip>
        </camera>
      </sensor>
    </link>
    <joint name="camera_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>camera_link</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- plugins -->
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher"
      filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system">
    </plugin>
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::OdometryPublisher"
      filename="gz-sim-odometry-publisher-system">
      <odom_frame>odom</odom_frame>
      <robot_base_frame>base_link</robot_base_frame>
      <dimensions>3</dimensions>
    </plugin>

    <!-- left/right motor Thruster plugins removed here: tracked variant isn't driven by props. -->

    <!-- Track-drive plugins: real gz-sim-track-controller-system (per-track belt speed) +
         gz-sim-tracked-vehicle-system (skid-steer body_link/left_track/right_track ->
         per-track velocity), parameter names/nesting empirically confirmed against gz-sim8's
         actual TrackedVehicle.cc source by Task 3's throwaway rig (see task-3-report.md).
         NOTE: Task 3 found body_link/left_track/right_track are NOT parsed uniformly --
         body_link is read as element TEXT content (_sdf->Get<std::string>("body_link")), but
         left_track/right_track are read from a `link` XML ATTRIBUTE
         (sdfElem->Get<std::string>("link")), not element text. Using text content for
         left_track/right_track (as an early attempt did) parses with no SDF-schema error and
         loads silently, but at runtime builds a malformed topic
         (.../link//track_cmd_vel -> sanitized to .../linktrack_cmd_vel) that nothing
         subscribes to -- commands are silently dropped. Fixed form below, verified via -v 4
         debug logs showing no "Failed to find left/right track" warnings. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-track-controller-system" name="gz::sim::systems::TrackController">
      <link>left_track</link>
      <track_orientation>1 0 0</track_orientation>
      <max_velocity>2.0</max_velocity>
      <max_acceleration>2.0</max_acceleration>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-track-controller-system" name="gz::sim::systems::TrackController">
      <link>right_track</link>
      <track_orientation>1 0 0</track_orientation>
      <max_velocity>2.0</max_velocity>
      <max_acceleration>2.0</max_acceleration>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-tracked-vehicle-system" name="gz::sim::systems::TrackedVehicle">
      <body_link>base_link</body_link>
      <left_track link="left_track"/>
      <right_track link="right_track"/>
      <tracks_separation>0.76</tracks_separation> <!-- left/right track mount y=+-0.35, plus the deployment struts' own +-0.03 outboard offset (see the strut comment above this whole assembly) = +-0.38 real track centerline -->
      <max_velocity>2.0</max_velocity>
      <max_acceleration>2.0</max_acceleration>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Retraction-joint actuation: real ros2_control + gz_ros2_control bridge, same pattern
         already proven on a prior project vehicle. position_proportional_gain is a
         GazeboSimSystem-wide SDF <plugin> child element, NOT a per-joint
         <ros2_control>/<param> -- confirmed the hard way there by reading gz_ros2_control's
         real upstream source. Reused directly here rather than rediscovered. -->
    <ros2_control name="GazeboSimSystem" type="system">
      <hardware>
        <plugin>gz_ros2_control/GazeboSimSystem</plugin>
      </hardware>
      <joint name="left_track_retract_joint">
        <command_interface name="position">
          <param name="min">0.0</param>
          <param name="max">1.4</param>
        </command_interface>
        <state_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
      </joint>
      <joint name="right_track_retract_joint">
        <command_interface name="position">
          <param name="min">0.0</param>
          <param name="max">1.4</param>
        </command_interface>
        <state_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
      </joint>
    </ros2_control>

    <plugin filename="gz_ros2_control-system" name="gz_ros2_control::GazeboSimROS2ControlPlugin">
      <!-- $(find pkg) is a xacro macro, not something gz_ros2_control expands: this is native
           SDF (no xacro preprocessing step), so gz_ros2_control::GazeboSimROS2ControlPlugin's
           Configure() passes <parameters> straight to rclcpp as a literal --params-file path.
           Confirmed the hard way -- `$(find cavex_tracked_vehicle)/config/...yaml` verbatim
           aborts the whole gz-sim process at model load (rclcpp::exceptions::RCLInvalidROSArgsError,
           "Error opening YAML file"). Using a real filesystem path instead, matching this
           project's existing convention of worktree-absolute paths (see ardupilot_gazebo_env.sh).
           ponytail: hardcoded to this worktree, not portable across machines/CI -- if this ever
           needs to run outside this checkout, template it (e.g. a launch file substituting
           FindPackageShare('cavex_tracked_vehicle') before spawning) instead of a literal path.
           Task 7 did exactly that: gazebo_tracked_vehicle.launch.py reads this checked-in file
           at launch time and replaces the placeholder token in the <parameters> tag below (see
           its literal value there -- not repeated here in the comment, since this file is
           processed with a whole-file string .replace() and a second literal occurrence here
           would get mangled too) with a real FindPackageShare('cavex_tracked_vehicle')-resolved
           path, writing the result to a
           generated temp file that is what actually gets spawned. This file itself is now a
           template (the token is not a valid path -- direct `gz sim model.sdf.tracked`
           throwaway loading, as Task 5 did for verification, needs the same substitution done
           by hand first, or just launch gazebo_tracked_vehicle.launch.py instead). -->
      <parameters>__ROS2_CONTROL_PARAMS_PATH__</parameters>
      <position_proportional_gain>20.0</position_proportional_gain>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Hydrodynamics plugin (gz-waves1-hydrodynamics-system) removed: real, root-caused
         finding, not a nicety. It's the original BlueBoat's water-buoyancy physics,
         irrelevant for this tracked, land-only variant that never enters water under its
         own power (Task 4 removed the motor/Thruster plugins for the same reason but missed
         this one). Its shared library isn't installed in this environment
         ("Failed to load system plugin [gz-waves1-hydrodynamics-system]: Could not find
         shared library", confirmed live in gz sim's own console output), and because it was
         listed immediately before ArduPilotPlugin in this file, its load failure was
         silently aborting ArduPilotPlugin's own loading too -- ArduPilotPlugin never
         initialized, so ArduPilot's DDS bridge (/ap/arm_motors, /ap/mode_switch) never had a
         live physics-side FDM connection behind it, explaining the "services visible in the
         ROS2 graph but every call hangs forever" symptom investigated at length across
         Tasks 11/12 and this dedicated investigation.
    -->

      <!-- ardupilot plugin
        The original BlueBoat's <control channel="..."> blocks (motor_port_joint /
        motor_stbd_joint, twin-thruster COMMAND channels) have been removed entirely: those
        joints no longer exist in this tracked variant (deleted above), and ArduPilotPlugin
        hard-fails at load ("Couldn't find specified joint") if a <control> block names a
        missing joint -- confirmed via a real spawn with libArduPilotPlugin.so. This is not a
        twin-motor boat anymore, so there is no physically-equivalent per-joint servo channel
        to repoint these at: skid-steer track velocity isn't a servo-angle/thrust output.
        ArduPilot itself still runs (IMU/NavSat in, no actuator commands out) so autopilot
        modes/estimation/telemetry keep working; actually driving the vehicle is Task 6's job,
        which relays ArduPilot's DDS cmd_vel output to the TrackedVehicle plugin's cmd_vel
        topic -- a separate control path that bypasses ArduPilotPlugin's servo channels
        entirely, so no <control> block is needed here at all. -->
    <plugin name="ArduPilotPlugin"
      filename="ArduPilotPlugin">
      <fdm_addr>127.0.0.1</fdm_addr>
      <fdm_port_in>9002</fdm_port_in>
      <connectionTimeoutMaxCount>5</connectionTimeoutMaxCount>
      <lock_step>1</lock_step>
      <!-- Real request: fix the "moving sideways not forward" bug at its
           root. Two live-tested attempts at THESE two parameters both
           failed for THAT symptom and were reverted at the time: (1) this
           term's yaw 90->0, (2) modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown's yaw
           0->90. Both left /ap/twist/filtered unchanged, (x=-0.03, y=0.40)
           for a commanded pure-forward (x=0.4). The REAL root cause of
           THAT symptom was found by reading ArduPilot's own AP_DDS source
           directly (ardupilot/libraries/AP_DDS/AP_DDS_Client.cpp,
           update_topic(TwistStamped&)): /ap/twist/filtered's linear.x/y is
           ahrs.get_velocity_NED() converted to ROS's ENU convention -- real
           WORLD-FRAME ground velocity (East/North), not body-frame
           forward/lateral, despite the message's own frame_id saying
           base_link. Fixed for real in track_cmd_vel_bridge.py: it now
           rotates that world-frame velocity into body-frame using the
           vehicle's LIVE yaw (from /ap/pose/filtered, same AHRS source),
           not a fixed constant -- verified correct end-to-end.

           SEPARATE bug, same two parameters, root-caused for real this
           session: ArduPilot's own reported ATTITUDE (yaw, /ap/pose/
           filtered) was found stuck at a highly stable but consistently
           wrong ~98-100deg offset from real ground truth, unaffected by
           EK3_SRC1_YAW (GPS-only vs GPS+compass fallback) -- the classic
           signature of a fixed upstream coordinate-transform bug, not an
           EKF convergence problem. Confirmed directly in the vendored
           ArduPilotPlugin's own source (ardupilot_gazebo/src/
           ArduPilotPlugin.cc, ~line 1843): the plugin's own TODO comment
           says wldAToWldG = gazeboXYZToNED.Inverse() "may be incorrect...
           not evident when using <gazeboXYZToNED>0 0 0 GZ_PI 0
           0</gazeboXYZToNED> but is when using... 0 0 0 GZ_PI 0 GZ_PI/2"
           -- i.e. .Inverse() is buggy specifically when gazeboXYZToNED
           has a non-zero YAW component. This vehicle's gazeboXYZToNED had
           yaw=90 (GZ_PI/2) -- exactly the flagged buggy case. Per the
           plugin's own math derivation comment (same file), gazeboXYZToNED
           is meant to be the UNIVERSAL, vehicle-independent Gazebo-ENU<->
           ArduPilot-NED relationship (yaw=0 always); any vehicle-specific
           body-mounting rotation belongs in
           modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown instead (the plugin's own
           Zephyr reference example does exactly this for its own 90deg
           yaw need) -- this vehicle had it backwards, in theory. TESTED
           FOR REAL this session (live-verified, unlike the earlier revert
           which only checked /ap/twist/filtered/velocity): swapped yaw
           from gazeboXYZToNED (90->0) to modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown
           (0->90), fresh eeprom, checked /ap/pose/filtered (attitude, the
           correct diagnostic for THIS bug) directly against ground truth.
           RESULT: zero measurable effect -- ArduPilot's own reported yaw
           was bit-for-bit identical (~97.17deg) both with and without the
           swap. Reverted back to the original values below (no benefit,
           and matches this file's own documented baseline). The TODO-
           flagged Inverse() bug in ArduPilotPlugin.cc is real (confirmed
           in the plugin's own source) but does not appear to be the cause
           of this specific frozen-heading symptom -- or fixing it alone
           isn't sufficient; the actual mechanism producing the stable-
           but-wrong ~97-100deg offset remains unexplained. Also tried:
           EK3_SRC1_YAW GPS-only vs GPS+compass-fallback, SIM_GPS1_LAG_MS/
           HZ tightening -- none of these three independent, plausible
           fixes moved this value at all, which is itself a strange,
           notable data point (suggests something more fundamental is
           holding the estimate fixed, not just under-corrected). NOT YET
           TRIED, DEFINITIVE RESULT: set AHRS_EKF_TYPE=10
           (AP_AHRS::EKFType::SIM) live via MAVLink param_set to bypass
           EKF/DCM entirely -- /ap/pose/filtered STILL reported ~96.9deg
           against real ground truth ~-1.6deg, with ZERO EKF/compass/GPS
           fusion involved at all. This proves conclusively the bug is
           NOT in EKF fusion logic (ruling out EK3_SRC1_YAW, GPS timing,
           and everything upstream of this point) -- it's baked into
           ArduPilot's raw SITL attitude state itself, fed directly by
           the Gazebo FDM interface (ArduPilotPlugin.cc). But the specific
           gazeboXYZToNED/modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown yaw-swap tested
           above did NOT fix it, so the real bug is somewhere else in
           that plugin's orientation-quaternion computation (possibly the
           FDM JSON's own "imu"/"quaternion" field construction, a
           separate code path from the wldAToBdyA Pose3d these two SDF
           params feed into) -- needs direct C++-level tracing of the
           plugin's actual quaternion output (add debug logging, rebuild,
           compare against known Gazebo ground truth at each step) to
           find the real bug. A bigger, riskier task than a config-only
           change -- left for a dedicated follow-up. AHRS_EKF_TYPE was
           reset back to 3 (EK3, normal) after this diagnostic.

           KNOWN OPEN ISSUE (direction-of-motion, DIFFERENT from the
           attitude-reporting bug above): even with a fully verified-
           correct command reaching the TrackedVehicle plugin, the
           vehicle's actual WORLD-frame motion direction was once measured
           at a consistent ~60deg offset from its own reported heading.
           This was FIXED this session (separately, see this file's own
           track collision <surface><friction> comment) -- root cause was
           missing anisotropic friction (fdir1) on the track collision
           boxes, not a coordinate-frame bug -- real motion direction is
           now correct (~5-14deg off-axis, live-verified). That fix and
           this one address two genuinely different problems that
           happened to look similar (both showed up as "wrong direction"
           symptoms): real motion direction (fixed via friction) vs.
           ArduPilot's own internal heading ESTIMATE (this fix). -->
      <gazeboXYZToNED degrees="true">0 0 0 180 0 90</gazeboXYZToNED>
      <modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown degrees="true">0 0 0 180 0 0</modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown>
      <imuName>imu_link::imu_sensor</imuName>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Motorized tether mount: the BlueROV2 is no longer rigidly carried
         (removed: the old rov_davit_link visual mount and the
         gz-sim-detachable-joint-system plugin that fixed it to the deck).
         Real reason for the change -- the old mechanism was a one-way rigid
         attach/detach (see vehicle_switch_node.py's prior docstring): the ROV
         either rode perfectly fixed to the hull or was permanently released,
         with no way to reel it back in. Replaced by
         motorized_tether_control.py, a real force-based constraint (see the
         gz-sim-apply-link-wrench-system world plugin in cavex_world.world)
         that pulls the ROV back within a commandable, motor-rate-limited
         payout length whenever the tether goes taut, and applies zero force
         when slack -- bidirectional, and the ROV can swim under its own
         thrusters the whole time, not just after a one-shot release. This
         tether_anchor_link is a small, static, visual-only cleat/spool
         fixture (opposite end of the hull from the helipad, moved 0.3m
         forward of an earlier x=-0.5 stern placement) marking the real
         attachment point motorized_tether_control.py computes its anchor
         pose from (base_link's hull collision bounding box is
         x:[-0.60,0.59] relative to base_link, per the mount-height
         derivation comment elsewhere in this file -- x=-0.2 stays well
         clear of both the stern edge and the bow-mounted helipad at
         x=0.3). Pure tether tension (no physical cradle) -- a caged
         version was tried and reverted: real collision-geometry bugs
         (the cage's own support surfaces ended up embedded inside the
         hull's own solid collision, unreachable) made it settle
         unpredictably rather than reliably, not worth the added
         complexity for now. -->
    <link name="tether_anchor_link">
      <pose relative_to="base_link">-0.2 0 0.05 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.2</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.001</ixx><iyy>0.001</iyy><izz>0.001</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="tether_anchor_visual">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.03</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.15 0.15 0.15 1</ambient><diffuse>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="tether_anchor_collision">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.03</radius><length>0.08</length></cylinder></geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>
    <joint name="tether_anchor_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>tether_anchor_link</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- Helipad, front (bow, +x) of the hull -- placed at x=0.3 (inboard of the
         camera mast at x=0.55/z=0.15, per that link's own real pose, to avoid
         visual/collision clash) so a real x500 quadcopter (vendored below, PX4's
         own Fuel model) has a visible landing platform. Deck-level (z=0.005,
         matching the hull collision's own real top surface). Flush again (no
         longer raised on a support post) -- that post existed only to clear a
         BlueROV2 mounted directly underneath it; the ROV has since moved to
         tether_anchor_link (see above, x=-0.2), which stays clear of this
         helipad's own footprint so the post is unneeded complexity. Purely a
         flat marked platform -- no elevator/lowering mechanism. -->
    <link name="helipad_link">
      <pose relative_to="base_link">0.3 0 0.005 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.5</mass>
        <inertia><ixx>0.01</ixx><iyy>0.01</iyy><izz>0.01</izz></inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="helipad_deck_visual">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.22</radius><length>0.01</length></cylinder></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.85 0.75 0.1 1</ambient><diffuse>0.9 0.8 0.15 1</diffuse></material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="helipad_deck_collision">
        <geometry><cylinder><radius>0.22</radius><length>0.01</length></cylinder></geometry>
      </collision>
    </link>
    <joint name="helipad_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>helipad_link</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- Carries a real, vendored PX4 x500 quadcopter (fuel.gazebosim.org/PX4/models/
         x500, CC-BY-4.0) on the helipad -- placeholder for future airpocket/aerial
         exploration work, NOT flight-control-integrated here. The vendored model
         itself has real motor/rotor physics (gz-sim-multicopter-motor-model-system)
         but no PX4 SITL/MAVLink connection wired up -- that needs the actual
         PX4-Autopilot SITL binary and its own Gazebo bridge, neither installed in
         this environment (the same class of toolchain-availability risk already
         hit and resolved for microxrceddsgen -- not repeated here since the
         drone's control integration is explicitly out of scope for now). Same
         real DetachableJoint mechanism as the ROV, so a real release path already
         exists for whenever flight control is added. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-detachable-joint-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::DetachableJoint">
      <parent_link>base_link</parent_link>
      <child_model>x500</child_model>
      <child_link>base_link</child_link>
      <detach_topic>/cavex/x500_release/detach</detach_topic>
    </plugin>

    <!-- ROV lock/unlock (real request): while in the dry cave section, the
         BlueROV2 is now RIGIDLY LOCKED to the hull -- not just held by
         tether tension (see the tether comment above; the tether stays
         active in parallel, and becomes the operative restraint again
         once unlocked, mimicking a real ROV's tether/umbilical staying
         connected even once it's released to operate). Real mechanism:
         the same gz-sim-detachable-joint-system plugin x500 already uses
         above, but with a real <attach_topic> too (confirmed via
         `strings` on the installed .so during an earlier session, same
         verification discipline as every other plugin in this project) --
         x500's own block never needed one because it spawns BEFORE the
         boat, so Configure() finds it and auto-attaches; bluerov2 spawns
         AFTER the boat (see the spawn-order comment in
         gazebo_tracked_vehicle.launch.py), so Configure() runs with no
         bluerov2 to find, and vehicle_switch_node.py has to explicitly
         publish to attach_topic once bluerov2 actually exists to
         establish the initial lock. Real release condition (per this
         session's request): vehicle_switch_node.py only unlocks once the
         boat has BOTH crossed the water boundary AND is genuinely afloat
         (not just past an x threshold) -- see that node's own comment for
         how "afloat" is determined, a real empirical finding, not
         assumed. -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-detachable-joint-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::DetachableJoint">
      <parent_link>base_link</parent_link>
      <child_model>bluerov2</child_model>
      <child_link>base_link</child_link>
      <attach_topic>/cavex/rov_lock/attach</attach_topic>
      <detach_topic>/cavex/rov_lock/detach</detach_topic>
    </plugin>

  </model>
</sdf>
